  "worked_solution": "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{49}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $49$ is a perfect square, we can get rid of the square root symbol ($\\sqrt{}$) and obtain a whole number: \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{49}=\\sqrt{{7}^{2}}=7\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{28}$ is a surd because $28$ is not a perfect square. In fact $\\sqrt{28}=5.291\\,502\\,622\\,...$\n\n- $\\sqrt{64}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $64$ is a perfect square, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a whole number:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{64}=\\sqrt{{8}^{2}}=8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{49}$ and $\\sqrt{64}$ only.",
